WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.836 --> 00:02.446 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference July 31,

00:02.446 --> 00:04.966 align:middle
2024 CHAIR POWELL.

00:05.026 --> 00:05.426 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:05.706 --> 00:07.946 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:07.946 --> 00:11.246 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:11.356 --> 00:14.136 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:15.146 --> 00:17.506 align:middle
Our economy has made
considerable progress toward

00:17.586 --> 00:19.616 align:middle
both goals over the
past two years.

00:20.606 --> 00:22.466 align:middle
The labor market has
come into better balance,

00:22.816 --> 00:24.566 align:middle
and the unemployment
rate remains low.

00:25.716 --> 00:27.986 align:middle
Inflation has eased
substantially from a peak

00:27.986 --> 00:30.266 align:middle
of 7 percent to 2.5 percent.

00:31.276 --> 00:33.366 align:middle
We are strongly committed
to returning inflation

00:33.366 --> 00:36.406 align:middle
to our 2 percent goal in
support of a strong economy

00:36.406 --> 00:37.716 align:middle
that benefits everyone.

00:39.446 --> 00:43.676 align:middle
Today, the FOMC decided to
leave our policy interest rate

00:43.676 --> 00:46.726 align:middle
unchanged and to continue to
reduce our securities holdings.

00:47.756 --> 00:49.516 align:middle
We are maintaining
our restrictive stance

00:49.516 --> 00:53.146 align:middle
of monetary policy in order to
keep demand in line with supply

00:53.646 --> 00:55.426 align:middle
and reduce inflationary
pressures.

00:56.536 --> 00:59.386 align:middle
We are attentive to risks on
both sides of our dual mandate,

00:59.726 --> 01:01.886 align:middle
and I will have more to
say about monetary policy

01:02.326 --> 01:04.776 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:06.486 --> 01:09.466 align:middle
Recent indicators suggest that
economic activity has continued

01:09.466 --> 01:11.326 align:middle
to expand at a solid pace.

01:12.326 --> 01:16.006 align:middle
GDP growth moderated to 2.1
percent in the first half

01:16.006 --> 01:19.156 align:middle
of the year, down from
3.1 percent last year.

01:20.526 --> 01:22.376 align:middle
Private domestic
final purchases,

01:22.376 --> 01:26.266 align:middle
or PDFP-which excludes inventory
investment, government spending,

01:26.266 --> 01:28.806 align:middle
and net exports and usually
sends a clearer signal

01:28.806 --> 01:32.636 align:middle
of underlying demand-grew
at a 2.6 percent pace

01:32.836 --> 01:34.626 align:middle
over that same period,
the first half.

01:35.756 --> 01:37.466 align:middle
Growth of consumer
spending has slowed

01:37.546 --> 01:40.246 align:middle
from last year's robust
pace but remains solid.

01:41.036 --> 01:43.326 align:middle
Investment in equipment
and intangibles has picked

01:43.326 --> 01:45.266 align:middle
up from its anemic
pace last year.

01:46.256 --> 01:48.616 align:middle
In the housing sector,
investment stalled

01:48.616 --> 01:51.206 align:middle
in the second quarter after
a strong rise in the first.

01:52.186 --> 01:55.116 align:middle
Improving supply conditions
have supported resilient demand

01:55.356 --> 01:56.396 align:middle
and the strong performance

01:56.396 --> 01:58.676 align:middle
of the U.S. economy
over the past year.

01:59.936 --> 02:02.116 align:middle
In the labor market, supply

02:02.116 --> 02:04.466 align:middle
and demand conditions have
come into better balance.

02:05.466 --> 02:09.266 align:middle
Payroll job gains averaged
177,000 jobs per month

02:09.426 --> 02:11.806 align:middle
in the second quarter-a
solid pace,

02:11.916 --> 02:13.666 align:middle
but below that seen
in the first quarter.

02:13.876 --> 02:16.436 align:middle
The unemployment
rate has moved up

02:16.686 --> 02:19.056 align:middle
but remains low at 4.1 percent.

02:20.756 --> 02:22.786 align:middle
Strong job creation
over the past couple

02:22.786 --> 02:26.206 align:middle
of years has been accompanied
by an increase in the supply

02:26.206 --> 02:28.216 align:middle
of workers, reflecting increases

02:28.216 --> 02:33.326 align:middle
in participation among
individuals aged 25 to 54 years

02:33.746 --> 02:35.486 align:middle
and a strong pace
of immigration.

02:36.716 --> 02:39.296 align:middle
Nominal wage growth has
eased over the past year,

02:39.526 --> 02:41.626 align:middle
and the jobs-to-workers
gap has narrowed.

02:43.066 --> 02:45.626 align:middle
Overall, a broad set
of indicators suggests

02:45.626 --> 02:49.086 align:middle
that conditions in the labor
market have returned to about

02:49.086 --> 02:50.246 align:middle
where they stood on the eve

02:50.376 --> 02:53.406 align:middle
of the pandemic-strong,
but not overheated.

02:55.496 --> 02:58.196 align:middle
Inflation has eased notably
over the past two years

02:58.196 --> 03:01.736 align:middle
but remains somewhat above our
longer-run goal of 2 percent.

03:03.436 --> 03:06.106 align:middle
Total PCE prices
rose 2.5 percent

03:06.106 --> 03:07.796 align:middle
over the 12 months
ending in June;

03:08.496 --> 03:10.936 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

03:10.936 --> 03:13.396 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
2.6 percent.

03:14.426 --> 03:16.466 align:middle
Longer-term inflation
expectations appear

03:16.466 --> 03:19.596 align:middle
to remain well anchored, as
reflected in a broad range

03:19.596 --> 03:23.436 align:middle
of surveys of households and
businesses and forecasters,

03:23.846 --> 03:25.746 align:middle
as well as measures
from financial markets.

03:27.256 --> 03:29.016 align:middle
My colleagues and
I are acutely aware

03:29.016 --> 03:31.376 align:middle
that high inflation imposes
significant hardship,

03:32.026 --> 03:35.626 align:middle
as it erodes purchasing power,
especially for those least able

03:35.626 --> 03:38.036 align:middle
to meet the higher costs
of essentials like food,

03:38.456 --> 03:39.846 align:middle
housing, and transportation.

03:41.166 --> 03:44.526 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

03:44.556 --> 03:47.176 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

03:47.176 --> 03:48.156 align:middle
for the American people.

03:49.016 --> 03:50.846 align:middle
In support of these goals,
the Committee decided

03:50.846 --> 03:53.396 align:middle
at today's meeting to
maintain the target range

03:53.436 --> 03:57.096 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate at 5¼ to 5½ percent

03:57.796 --> 03:59.936 align:middle
and to continue reducing
our securities holdings.

04:01.036 --> 04:03.946 align:middle
As the labor market has cooled
and inflation has declined,

04:04.676 --> 04:06.396 align:middle
the risks to achieving
our employment

04:06.396 --> 04:09.576 align:middle
and inflation goals continue
to move into better balance.

04:10.496 --> 04:12.116 align:middle
Indeed, we're attentive
to the risks

04:12.176 --> 04:13.726 align:middle
to both sides of
our dual mandate.

04:13.836 --> 04:18.366 align:middle
We've stated that we do not
expect it will be appropriate

04:18.366 --> 04:20.546 align:middle
to reduce the target range
for the federal funds rate

04:20.886 --> 04:22.816 align:middle
until we have gained
greater confidence

04:22.816 --> 04:25.896 align:middle
that inflation is moving
sustainably toward 2 percent.

04:26.216 --> 04:29.296 align:middle
The second quarter's
inflation readings have added

04:29.296 --> 04:32.406 align:middle
to our confidence, and more good
data would further strengthen

04:32.406 --> 04:33.166 align:middle
that confidence.

04:34.246 --> 04:36.436 align:middle
We will continue to make our
decisions meeting by meeting.

04:36.866 --> 04:39.366 align:middle
We know that reducing
policy restraint too soon

04:39.366 --> 04:41.906 align:middle
or too much could
result in a reversal

04:42.006 --> 04:43.796 align:middle
of the progress we
have seen on inflation.

04:44.476 --> 04:47.806 align:middle
At the same time, reducing
policy restraint too late

04:47.806 --> 04:50.896 align:middle
or too little could unduly
weaken economic activity

04:50.896 --> 04:51.596 align:middle
and employment.

04:53.096 --> 04:55.286 align:middle
In considering any
adjustments to the target range

04:55.286 --> 04:56.266 align:middle
for the federal funds rate,

04:56.566 --> 04:58.836 align:middle
the Committee will carefully
assess incoming data,

04:59.356 --> 05:02.196 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
and the balance of risks.

05:03.946 --> 05:07.366 align:middle
As the economy evolves, monetary
policy will adjust in order

05:07.366 --> 05:09.256 align:middle
to best promote our
maximum-employment

05:09.256 --> 05:10.596 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

05:11.586 --> 05:14.246 align:middle
If the economy remains solid
and inflation persists,

05:14.686 --> 05:16.706 align:middle
we can maintain the
current target range

05:16.706 --> 05:18.816 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
as long as appropriate.

05:19.796 --> 05:22.066 align:middle
If the labor market were
to weaken unexpectedly

05:22.096 --> 05:24.406 align:middle
or inflation were to fall
more quickly than anticipated,

05:25.266 --> 05:26.456 align:middle
we are prepared to respond.

05:27.286 --> 05:30.096 align:middle
Policy is well positioned
to deal with the risks

05:30.096 --> 05:31.346 align:middle
and uncertainties that we face

05:31.586 --> 05:33.706 align:middle
in pursuing both sides
of our dual mandate.

05:34.966 --> 05:36.806 align:middle
The Fed has been
assigned two goals

05:36.806 --> 05:40.146 align:middle
for monetary policy-maximum
employment and stable prices.

05:40.806 --> 05:42.956 align:middle
We remain committed to
bringing inflation back

05:42.956 --> 05:44.336 align:middle
down to our 2 percent goal

05:44.776 --> 05:47.276 align:middle
and to keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

05:47.336 --> 05:48.016 align:middle
well anchored.

05:49.256 --> 05:51.046 align:middle
Restoring price stability
is essential

05:51.046 --> 05:52.636 align:middle
to achieving maximum employment

05:52.636 --> 05:56.316 align:middle
and stable prices
over the longer run.

05:56.476 --> 05:57.556 align:middle
Our success in delivering

05:57.556 --> 05:59.696 align:middle
on these goals matters
to all Americans.

06:00.286 --> 06:03.856 align:middle
We understand that our actions
affect communities, families,

06:03.946 --> 06:05.616 align:middle
and businesses across
the country.

06:06.646 --> 06:09.096 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

06:09.896 --> 06:11.556 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

06:11.746 --> 06:13.466 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

06:13.546 --> 06:14.776 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

06:15.456 --> 06:15.856 align:middle
Thank you.

06:16.096 --> 06:17.296 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

06:17.696 --> 06:18.506 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

06:19.946 --> 06:21.906 align:middle
Jeanna. JEANNA SMIALEK.

06:22.356 --> 06:25.076 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek from
the New York Times.

06:25.076 --> 06:26.446 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

06:26.976 --> 06:29.676 align:middle
Markets pretty much
entirely expect a rate cut

06:29.676 --> 06:31.386 align:middle
in September at this stage.

06:31.796 --> 06:34.556 align:middle
I wonder if you think that's
a reasonable expectation, and,

06:34.636 --> 06:38.116 align:middle
if so, why not just
make the move today?

06:38.116 --> 06:38.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

06:38.656 --> 06:38.926 align:middle
Thank you.

06:38.926 --> 06:41.946 align:middle
So on September,
let me say this.

06:41.946 --> 06:44.766 align:middle
We, we have made no decisions
about future meetings,

06:44.766 --> 06:46.146 align:middle
and that includes the
September meeting.

06:47.056 --> 06:49.566 align:middle
The broad sense of
the Committee is

06:49.566 --> 06:51.706 align:middle
that the economy is
moving closer to the point

06:51.706 --> 06:54.066 align:middle
at which it will be appropriate
to reduce our policy rate.

06:55.266 --> 06:56.956 align:middle
In that, we will
be data dependent

06:56.996 --> 06:59.946 align:middle
but not data point dependent,
so it will not be a question

06:59.946 --> 07:03.656 align:middle
of responding specifically
to one or two data releases.

07:04.096 --> 07:07.266 align:middle
The question will be whether
the totality of the data,

07:07.536 --> 07:11.046 align:middle
the evolving outlook, and the
balance of risks are consistent

07:11.046 --> 07:12.776 align:middle
with rising confidence
on inflation

07:12.776 --> 07:14.446 align:middle
and maintaining a
solid labor market.

07:15.066 --> 07:18.726 align:middle
If that test is met, a reduction
in our policy rate could be

07:18.726 --> 07:21.296 align:middle
on the table as soon as the
next meeting in September.

07:22.626 --> 07:24.236 align:middle
So you asked, why not today?

07:24.486 --> 07:27.726 align:middle
And I would just say,
again, that the broad sense

07:27.726 --> 07:30.266 align:middle
of the Committee is that we're
getting closer to the point

07:30.266 --> 07:32.266 align:middle
at which it will be appropriate
to reduce our policy rate

07:32.826 --> 07:34.726 align:middle
but that we're not
quite at that point yet.

07:35.486 --> 07:36.106 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:36.106 --> 07:39.706 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

07:40.826 --> 07:44.726 align:middle
So just to, to follow up on
that a bit, if inflation behaves

07:44.726 --> 07:47.366 align:middle
as you expect between
now and September,

07:47.366 --> 07:49.436 align:middle
would you regard a cut
in September as sort

07:49.436 --> 07:51.576 align:middle
of the baseline scenario
right now?

07:52.096 --> 07:52.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:52.876 --> 07:55.066 align:middle
So I guess I would-I would
think about it this way.

07:55.266 --> 07:58.196 align:middle
I'll give an example of,

07:58.196 --> 08:00.836 align:middle
of cases in which it would be
appropriate to cut and maybe

08:00.926 --> 08:02.226 align:middle
that it wouldn't be
appropriate to cut.

08:02.226 --> 08:07.416 align:middle
So if we were to see, for
example, inflation moving

08:07.486 --> 08:11.606 align:middle
down quickly or more or less
in line with expectations,

08:12.036 --> 08:15.636 align:middle
growth remains, let's
say, reasonably strong,

08:16.206 --> 08:18.676 align:middle
and the labor market
remains, you know,

08:19.196 --> 08:21.646 align:middle
consistent with its current
condition, then I would think

08:22.006 --> 08:24.676 align:middle
that a, a rate cut
could be on the table

08:24.816 --> 08:26.016 align:middle
at the September meeting.

08:26.666 --> 08:30.116 align:middle
If, if inflation were to prove,
you know, sticky and we were

08:30.116 --> 08:31.666 align:middle
to see higher readings
from inflation,

08:31.666 --> 08:34.496 align:middle
disappointing readings,
we would weigh that along

08:34.496 --> 08:35.426 align:middle
with the other things.

08:35.496 --> 08:38.206 align:middle
You know, I think it's going
to be not just any one thing.

08:38.206 --> 08:40.086 align:middle
It's going to be
the inflation data,

08:40.086 --> 08:43.316 align:middle
it's going to be
the employment data,

08:43.316 --> 08:45.986 align:middle
it's going to be the balance of
risks as we see it-it's going

08:45.986 --> 08:47.906 align:middle
to be the totality
of all of that

08:48.096 --> 08:49.536 align:middle
that would help us
make this decision.

08:49.536 --> 08:49.906 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

08:49.976 --> 08:52.196 align:middle
And just to follow up
on that-specifically,

08:52.666 --> 08:55.206 align:middle
in what ways right now,
given all you've seen

08:55.206 --> 08:57.736 align:middle
over the last few months,
in particular on shelter,

08:57.736 --> 09:01.266 align:middle
on services, et cetera, in what
ways are you not confident right

09:01.266 --> 09:03.856 align:middle
now that inflation is on
the way back to 2 percent?

09:03.856 --> 09:04.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:04.156 --> 09:06.476 align:middle
I, I think it's just a question
of seeing more good data.

09:06.656 --> 09:08.716 align:middle
We have seen-the last
couple of readings have,

09:08.806 --> 09:10.306 align:middle
have certainly added
to confidence.

09:10.306 --> 09:13.366 align:middle
And we've seen progress
across all three categories

09:13.456 --> 09:16.446 align:middle
of core PCE inflation-that's
goods,

09:16.446 --> 09:18.366 align:middle
nonhousing services,
and housing services.

09:18.856 --> 09:22.296 align:middle
So it's really just-you
know, we had a quarter

09:22.366 --> 09:24.606 align:middle
of poor inflation data at
the beginning of the year.

09:24.606 --> 09:26.606 align:middle
Then we saw some more
good inflation data;

09:26.606 --> 09:28.806 align:middle
we had seven months at
the end of last year.

09:29.346 --> 09:32.476 align:middle
You know, we just want to see
more and, and gain confidence.

09:32.476 --> 09:35.126 align:middle
And, as I said, we have-we
did gain confidence,

09:35.346 --> 09:38.646 align:middle
and more good data would cause
us to, to gain more confidence.

09:38.736 --> 09:39.396 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

09:39.836 --> 09:40.776 align:middle
Thank you.

09:40.776 --> 09:42.696 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:42.976 --> 09:43.536 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

09:43.956 --> 09:44.426 align:middle
Thank you.

09:44.426 --> 09:45.796 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the Financial Times.

09:46.186 --> 09:49.266 align:middle
The March SEP pointed
to three cuts in 2024,

09:49.266 --> 09:51.056 align:middle
with core inflation
at 2.6 percent

09:51.056 --> 09:52.756 align:middle
and the unemployment
rate at 4 percent.

09:52.756 --> 09:55.086 align:middle
Since we're now at that
level in terms of inflation

09:55.086 --> 09:57.746 align:middle
and already beyond what was
projected for the labor market,

09:57.746 --> 09:59.906 align:middle
I'm just wondering if
that rate path is back

10:00.036 --> 10:03.386 align:middle
to being the best guidepost for
policy rather than, let's say,

10:03.386 --> 10:05.406 align:middle
the shallower one laid
out in that June SEP.

10:05.406 --> 10:06.086 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:07.336 --> 10:11.956 align:middle
You know, so I would just say,
really, the path ahead is going

10:11.956 --> 10:14.426 align:middle
to depend on the way
the economy evolves.

10:14.866 --> 10:16.416 align:middle
And I can't really give you any,

10:16.486 --> 10:19.026 align:middle
any better forward
guidance on it than that.

10:19.026 --> 10:21.296 align:middle
We didn't, of course, do
an SEP at this meeting.

10:21.296 --> 10:23.496 align:middle
We will do another one
at the September meeting.

10:24.186 --> 10:27.096 align:middle
I would just say, I can-I
can imagine a scenario

10:27.096 --> 10:30.956 align:middle
in which there would be
everywhere from zero cuts

10:30.956 --> 10:34.346 align:middle
to several cuts, depending on
the way the economy evolves,

10:34.476 --> 10:36.136 align:middle
and I wouldn't want to lay

10:36.136 --> 10:38.446 align:middle
out a baseline path
for you there today.

10:39.076 --> 10:41.956 align:middle
I've said what I-what I
can say about September

10:41.956 --> 10:42.626 align:middle
and about today, though.

10:42.626 --> 10:44.956 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:44.956 --> 10:47.776 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

10:49.786 --> 10:51.196 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

10:51.886 --> 10:53.886 align:middle
Chair Powell, you've said
before that you wouldn't wait

10:53.886 --> 10:56.356 align:middle
until inflation got to
2 percent to cut rates

10:56.426 --> 10:58.096 align:middle
because of how inflation
has lagged.

10:58.736 --> 11:01.296 align:middle
Does that apply for
the labor market, too?

11:01.346 --> 11:04.556 align:middle
If the labor market is
back in equilibrium,

11:04.656 --> 11:07.056 align:middle
why is restrictive
policy and, potentially,

11:07.056 --> 11:08.266 align:middle
very restrictive policy,

11:08.266 --> 11:11.426 align:middle
given the high real funds
rate, warranted right now?

11:12.356 --> 11:13.166 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:13.346 --> 11:15.286 align:middle
So this is the very reason

11:15.286 --> 11:19.086 align:middle
that we're thinking
about-that we've said

11:19.086 --> 11:21.776 align:middle
in our-in our statement that
we're going back to looking

11:21.776 --> 11:24.526 align:middle
at both mandates and that we
think the risks are coming back

11:24.526 --> 11:25.146 align:middle
into balance.

11:25.776 --> 11:27.426 align:middle
We think what the
data broadly show

11:27.426 --> 11:31.046 align:middle
in the labor market is an
ongoing, gradual normalization

11:31.046 --> 11:32.086 align:middle
of labor market conditions.

11:32.086 --> 11:32.906 align:middle
And that's what we want to see.

11:32.906 --> 11:35.256 align:middle
You know, we've seen that over
a period of a couple of years

11:35.916 --> 11:38.536 align:middle
and a move, really, from
overheated conditions

11:38.536 --> 11:39.906 align:middle
to more normal conditions.

11:40.486 --> 11:44.696 align:middle
We are watching the labor
market conditions quite closely,

11:44.846 --> 11:46.366 align:middle
and that's what we're seeing.

11:46.366 --> 11:49.306 align:middle
If we-if we start to see
something that looks to be more

11:49.306 --> 11:51.646 align:middle
than that, then we're well
positioned to respond.

11:52.196 --> 11:53.556 align:middle
That's, that's part of
what we're thinking.

11:54.176 --> 11:54.636 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

11:54.636 --> 11:58.106 align:middle
And when you talk about
seeing something that's more

11:58.106 --> 12:00.896 align:middle
than whatever softness
or slowdown you expect,

12:02.416 --> 12:06.406 align:middle
in the past you've said that
stronger growth wouldn't,

12:06.626 --> 12:09.176 align:middle
wouldn't override
better news on inflation.

12:09.176 --> 12:11.326 align:middle
I wonder how that
cuts the other way.

12:11.386 --> 12:14.276 align:middle
If you're seeing more
softness in the labor market

12:14.276 --> 12:18.326 align:middle
than what you would expect,
does that change the calculus

12:18.326 --> 12:21.066 align:middle
on what you're looking for
out of the inflation numbers

12:21.486 --> 12:22.666 align:middle
to recalibrate policy?

12:22.666 --> 12:23.406 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:23.456 --> 12:26.166 align:middle
So we have, we have-growth
isn't one of our three.

12:26.166 --> 12:27.976 align:middle
We have two mandates,
as you know.

12:27.976 --> 12:31.066 align:middle
The labor market,
maximum employment is one,

12:31.066 --> 12:32.226 align:middle
and stable prices is another.

12:32.226 --> 12:36.376 align:middle
So we weigh-we weigh those two
things equally under the law.

12:36.806 --> 12:39.206 align:middle
When we were far away from
our inflation mandate,

12:39.206 --> 12:40.156 align:middle
we had to focus on that.

12:40.156 --> 12:42.596 align:middle
Now we're back, back to
a closer to even focus,

12:42.596 --> 12:45.296 align:middle
so we'll be looking at
labor market conditions

12:45.296 --> 12:47.556 align:middle
and asking whether we're
getting what we're seeing.

12:47.636 --> 12:50.296 align:middle
And, as I said, we're
prepared to respond if we see

12:50.296 --> 12:53.136 align:middle
that it's-that it's not what
we wanted to see, which was,

12:53.516 --> 12:55.006 align:middle
you know, a gradual
normalization

12:55.006 --> 12:56.596 align:middle
of conditions-if we
see more than that.

12:56.596 --> 12:59.326 align:middle
And it wouldn't be any one
statistic, although, of course,

12:59.326 --> 13:01.716 align:middle
the unemployment rate is
generally thought to be,

13:02.216 --> 13:04.406 align:middle
you know, a single-a
good single statistic.

13:04.476 --> 13:06.906 align:middle
But we'd be looking at
wages, we'd be looking

13:06.906 --> 13:08.016 align:middle
at participation,
we'd be looking

13:08.016 --> 13:11.606 align:middle
at all the things-surveys,
quits, hires,

13:11.606 --> 13:13.956 align:middle
all of those things-to
determine the overall status

13:13.956 --> 13:14.646 align:middle
of the labor market.

13:14.646 --> 13:15.926 align:middle
But we're looking at it now.

13:16.526 --> 13:20.026 align:middle
I would say, again, I think
you're back to conditions

13:20.026 --> 13:22.986 align:middle
that are close to
2019 conditions,

13:23.056 --> 13:27.066 align:middle
and that was not an inflationary
economy-broadly similar labor

13:27.066 --> 13:27.736 align:middle
markets then.

13:28.156 --> 13:30.306 align:middle
I think inflation was
actually-core inflation was

13:30.306 --> 13:31.686 align:middle
actually running
below 2 percent.

13:31.686 --> 13:34.216 align:middle
So we don't think-I don't
now think of the labor market

13:34.216 --> 13:37.496 align:middle
in its current state
as a likely source

13:38.006 --> 13:40.686 align:middle
of significant inflationary
pressures.

13:41.036 --> 13:45.516 align:middle
So I would-I would not like to
see a material further cooling

13:45.516 --> 13:47.436 align:middle
in the labor market,
and that's part

13:47.436 --> 13:48.526 align:middle
of what's behind our thinking.

13:48.526 --> 13:49.396 align:middle
The other part, of course,

13:49.396 --> 13:52.436 align:middle
is that we have made real
progress on inflation.

13:52.436 --> 13:55.006 align:middle
And we're-we've got
growing confidence there

13:55.556 --> 13:59.256 align:middle
that we are not quite there
yet, but we're more confident

13:59.286 --> 14:00.396 align:middle
that we're on a-that we're

14:00.396 --> 14:02.906 align:middle
on a sustainable path
down to 2 percent.

14:02.906 --> 14:04.576 align:middle
So those two things
are working together,

14:04.576 --> 14:06.716 align:middle
and we're factoring those
both into our policy.

14:08.006 --> 14:08.606 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:09.486 --> 14:10.666 align:middle
Chris. CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

14:10.946 --> 14:12.596 align:middle
Chris Rugaber at
Associated Press.

14:13.966 --> 14:15.176 align:middle
You mentioned not wanting

14:15.176 --> 14:17.486 align:middle
to see any further
cooling in the job market.

14:17.486 --> 14:22.266 align:middle
Why not? Or would you consider
preemptive cuts to prevent

14:22.816 --> 14:24.986 align:middle
if you saw risks of
an unexpected cooling?

14:24.986 --> 14:27.166 align:middle
Is that something you would
cut ahead of time for?

14:27.486 --> 14:27.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:27.986 --> 14:32.096 align:middle
So I wouldn't say I wouldn't
want to see any other cooling.

14:32.096 --> 14:33.746 align:middle
It would be more of a
material difference.

14:33.746 --> 14:36.346 align:middle
If we-if we'd be looking at
this and if we see something

14:36.346 --> 14:38.186 align:middle
that looks like a more
significant downturn,

14:38.726 --> 14:42.936 align:middle
that would be something that
we would-we would have the

14:43.366 --> 14:44.686 align:middle
intention of responding to.

14:46.786 --> 14:50.076 align:middle
So, in terms of-I don't
think of it that way.

14:50.076 --> 14:51.206 align:middle
I think of it as, you know,

14:51.206 --> 14:52.706 align:middle
we're actually in
a good place here.

14:52.706 --> 14:56.166 align:middle
We're balancing these two risks
of, you know, go too soon,

14:56.486 --> 14:59.966 align:middle
and you undermine progress
on inflation; wait too long

14:59.966 --> 15:02.656 align:middle
or don't go fast enough, and,

15:02.656 --> 15:04.666 align:middle
and you put at risk
the recovery.

15:05.276 --> 15:06.886 align:middle
And so we have to
balance those two things.

15:06.886 --> 15:08.856 align:middle
That's the nature of
having two mandates,

15:09.086 --> 15:11.336 align:middle
and I think we-this is
how we balance them.

15:11.966 --> 15:15.206 align:middle
It's a rough balance, but
it does feel like, again,

15:15.206 --> 15:16.846 align:middle
the labor market feels
like it's in a place

15:16.846 --> 15:20.576 align:middle
where it's just a process
of ongoing normalization;

15:20.576 --> 15:24.126 align:middle
4.1 percent unemployment
is still historically low.

15:24.756 --> 15:28.096 align:middle
And, you know, we'll just have
to see what the data show us.

15:28.096 --> 15:28.566 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

15:28.856 --> 15:29.926 align:middle
And, just to follow up quickly:

15:30.176 --> 15:31.576 align:middle
[I] wanted to see
what you thought

15:31.576 --> 15:34.246 align:middle
of the recent JOLTS report,
which did show hiring,

15:34.246 --> 15:37.296 align:middle
gross hiring, has come down
even below 2019 levels.

15:38.016 --> 15:39.226 align:middle
Layoffs remain low.

15:39.706 --> 15:42.636 align:middle
So it painted a picture of
a very static labor market.

15:43.146 --> 15:47.136 align:middle
Is that sustainable in your view
or something that is worrying?

15:47.486 --> 15:47.696 align:middle
Thank you.

15:47.696 --> 15:47.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:47.986 --> 15:51.116 align:middle
So I think all of the data
points continue to point to kind

15:51.116 --> 15:52.506 align:middle
of the direction we
would want to see.

15:52.506 --> 15:53.776 align:middle
So that was taken as, you know,

15:54.346 --> 15:56.256 align:middle
there was a decline
in job openings.

15:56.816 --> 15:57.556 align:middle
That was good.

15:57.556 --> 16:02.426 align:middle
Today's ECI reading was a little
softer than expected, so that's,

16:02.906 --> 16:04.236 align:middle
that's a good reading.

16:04.236 --> 16:08.206 align:middle
It shows that wage increases are
still at a strong level but that

16:08.206 --> 16:09.566 align:middle
that level continues to come

16:09.566 --> 16:12.226 align:middle
down to more sustainable
levels over time.

16:12.226 --> 16:14.376 align:middle
That's exactly the pattern
that we want to be seeing.

16:14.996 --> 16:17.156 align:middle
So I think the data-the
data we've been seeing

16:17.156 --> 16:19.126 align:middle
in the labor market
are broadly consistent

16:19.126 --> 16:20.856 align:middle
with that normalization process.

16:21.276 --> 16:24.606 align:middle
Again, we're closely monitoring
to see whether it starts

16:24.606 --> 16:27.496 align:middle
to show signs that
it's more than that.

16:27.936 --> 16:28.436 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:29.346 --> 16:30.606 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

16:30.886 --> 16:32.046 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

16:32.046 --> 16:36.426 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, back in March,
you talked about cutting rates

16:36.476 --> 16:39.746 align:middle
as a process, and, in June,
you talked about the idea that,

16:39.746 --> 16:41.576 align:middle
well, one rate cut
wouldn't do anything.

16:41.576 --> 16:44.746 align:middle
So I wonder if you
can sort of-following

16:44.746 --> 16:47.466 align:middle
up on Colby's question-talk
about,

16:48.386 --> 16:52.246 align:middle
are you weighing the
economy right now in terms

16:52.246 --> 16:54.676 align:middle
of its ability to
withstand multiple rate cuts?

16:55.016 --> 16:56.176 align:middle
Talk us through the process

16:56.176 --> 16:58.466 align:middle
that you think-or is
it just one rate cut?

16:58.466 --> 17:00.316 align:middle
Or are you in the process now
of thinking that rates need

17:00.316 --> 17:01.416 align:middle
to be normalized here?

17:01.626 --> 17:01.976 align:middle
Thank you.

17:03.446 --> 17:04.476 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:04.596 --> 17:07.036 align:middle
Yeah, I can't really
say that, honestly.

17:07.036 --> 17:11.436 align:middle
You know, we're-we-we've
seen significant movement

17:11.436 --> 17:14.316 align:middle
in the labor market, and,
you know, we're very mindful

17:14.316 --> 17:17.476 align:middle
of this question of, is it just
normalization or is it more?

17:17.476 --> 17:19.896 align:middle
We think it's just
normalization, but we want to be

17:19.896 --> 17:22.296 align:middle
in a position to, to
support the labor market.

17:22.296 --> 17:24.286 align:middle
At the same time, we're
seeing progress on inflation.

17:24.286 --> 17:26.976 align:middle
So, you know, we actually got

17:26.976 --> 17:30.786 align:middle
to this-we raised rates a
year ago at the July meeting.

17:31.186 --> 17:33.586 align:middle
And if you look at the situation
in the economy a year ago,

17:34.286 --> 17:38.316 align:middle
unemployment-sorry,
inflation was over 4 percent.

17:38.846 --> 17:40.186 align:middle
It was a completely
different economy.

17:40.186 --> 17:41.586 align:middle
Now we've made a
lot of progress,

17:41.586 --> 17:45.576 align:middle
and the labor market-I think
unemployment was in the 3s,

17:45.576 --> 17:47.956 align:middle
mid-3s, so it's a
different economy.

17:47.956 --> 17:51.136 align:middle
And I think it's time,
it's coming to be time,

17:51.136 --> 17:55.436 align:middle
to adjust that so that we
support this continued process.

17:55.436 --> 18:00.666 align:middle
The thing we're trying to
do is-you know that we have,

18:00.666 --> 18:02.546 align:middle
we've had this really
significant decline

18:02.636 --> 18:05.816 align:middle
in inflation, and
unemployment has remained low.

18:05.986 --> 18:08.846 align:middle
And this is a really
unusual and historically,

18:09.466 --> 18:12.336 align:middle
historically unusual and
such a welcome outcome

18:12.336 --> 18:13.196 align:middle
for the people we serve.

18:13.706 --> 18:15.586 align:middle
What we're thinking
about all the time is,

18:15.886 --> 18:16.946 align:middle
how do we keep this going?

18:16.996 --> 18:18.356 align:middle
And this is-this
is part of that.

18:18.726 --> 18:22.956 align:middle
We think we don't need to be
100 percent focused on inflation

18:22.956 --> 18:26.556 align:middle
because of the progress
we've made: 12-month headline

18:26.726 --> 18:28.376 align:middle
at 2½ [percent], core at 2.6.

18:29.036 --> 18:30.796 align:middle
You know, it's way
down from where it was.

18:30.896 --> 18:33.376 align:middle
The job is not done on
inflation, but, nonetheless,

18:33.896 --> 18:36.556 align:middle
we can afford to begin to
dial back the restriction

18:36.556 --> 18:37.546 align:middle
in our policy rate.

18:38.176 --> 18:39.946 align:middle
And I think we're
just-it's part of a process.

18:39.946 --> 18:42.486 align:middle
In terms of what that
looks like, I mean,

18:42.486 --> 18:46.616 align:middle
I think most rate-you would
think, in a base case,

18:47.176 --> 18:49.936 align:middle
that policy rates would move
down from here, but I don't want

18:49.936 --> 18:53.956 align:middle
to try to give specific
forward guidance about when

18:53.956 --> 18:56.466 align:middle
that might be, the pace
at which it might happen,

18:56.466 --> 18:58.586 align:middle
because I think that's really
going to depend on the economy,

18:58.586 --> 18:59.556 align:middle
and that's highly uncertain.

18:59.556 --> 19:00.716 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:00.916 --> 19:02.646 align:middle
Rachel. RACHEL SIEGEL.

19:03.796 --> 19:05.216 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

19:05.216 --> 19:06.546 align:middle
Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

19:06.546 --> 19:07.786 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

19:08.856 --> 19:12.376 align:middle
On inflation, do the past few
months of good reports look

19:12.376 --> 19:14.466 align:middle
like what we saw last year,
where you really had a lot

19:14.466 --> 19:16.336 align:middle
of momentum, with a
few bumps in between?

19:16.876 --> 19:19.366 align:middle
Would you characterize that kind
of momentum as back on track

19:19.366 --> 19:20.116 align:middle
at this point in the year?

19:20.786 --> 19:21.056 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:21.056 --> 19:22.636 align:middle
Actually, what we're seeing
now is a little better

19:22.636 --> 19:23.616 align:middle
than what we saw last year.

19:23.616 --> 19:26.096 align:middle
Last year, as we pointed
out late in the year,

19:26.366 --> 19:28.456 align:middle
a whole lot of the progress
we saw last year was

19:28.646 --> 19:30.376 align:middle
from goods prices,
which were going

19:30.376 --> 19:32.766 align:middle
down at an unsustainable
rate, disinflating

19:32.896 --> 19:34.086 align:middle
at an unsustainable rate.

19:34.686 --> 19:36.516 align:middle
This is a broader disinflation.

19:36.606 --> 19:38.926 align:middle
This has goods prices
coming down,

19:38.926 --> 19:41.156 align:middle
but it's also-we're
also now seeing progress

19:41.156 --> 19:42.786 align:middle
in the other two big categories:

19:43.236 --> 19:45.086 align:middle
nonhousing services
and housing services.

19:45.086 --> 19:46.916 align:middle
So, you know, the thing is,

19:46.916 --> 19:49.406 align:middle
we've only-you've got
one quarter of that.

19:49.406 --> 19:51.536 align:middle
We had seven months
of low inflation;

19:51.896 --> 19:53.006 align:middle
you got one quarter of this.

19:53.006 --> 19:56.686 align:middle
I would say the quality of this
is, is higher, and it's good,

19:56.816 --> 19:58.116 align:middle
but so far, it's only a quarter.

19:58.496 --> 20:01.766 align:middle
So I think, you know, we
need to see more to know

20:01.766 --> 20:04.646 align:middle
that we're-to have more
confidence that we're on,

20:05.176 --> 20:06.726 align:middle
on a good path down
to 2 percent.

20:06.726 --> 20:08.586 align:middle
But as I mentioned, our
confidence is growing

20:08.586 --> 20:10.056 align:middle
because we've been
getting good data.

20:10.536 --> 20:14.196 align:middle
And things like the ECI report
and, frankly, the softening

20:14.196 --> 20:16.846 align:middle
in the labor market
conditions, you know,

20:16.846 --> 20:19.976 align:middle
give you more confidence that
the economy's not overheating.

20:19.976 --> 20:21.756 align:middle
It doesn't look like
an overheating economy,

20:22.346 --> 20:25.636 align:middle
and it looks like an
economy that's normalizing.

20:25.816 --> 20:26.966 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

20:26.966 --> 20:29.126 align:middle
And if we're to think about the
first couple months of the year,

20:29.446 --> 20:32.006 align:middle
is there any sense now
that they were these blips

20:32.006 --> 20:34.426 align:middle
that could have actually
allowed for earlier rate cuts

20:34.426 --> 20:36.636 align:middle
as were some of the
projections going into 2024?

20:36.636 --> 20:36.846 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:36.846 --> 20:39.196 align:middle
So the thing about-if
what it is is seasonality,

20:39.196 --> 20:42.046 align:middle
and it could just be-it's
very, very hard to, you know,

20:42.146 --> 20:44.886 align:middle
to do appropriate
seasonal adjustments.

20:45.056 --> 20:47.796 align:middle
If that's what it is, then
that actually implies that,

20:48.106 --> 20:50.676 align:middle
that other months were
underreporting too

20:50.676 --> 20:51.466 align:middle
low inflation.

20:51.466 --> 20:53.646 align:middle
If you smoothed it out,
it's a zero-sum game.

20:53.956 --> 20:55.286 align:middle
And that's why we
look at 12 months.

20:55.696 --> 20:58.336 align:middle
We look at 12 months because
that takes all of that out,

20:58.336 --> 20:59.016 align:middle
all those effects out.

20:59.436 --> 21:03.126 align:middle
Twelve-month now is 2½ percent
headline, 2.6 percent core.

21:03.856 --> 21:06.876 align:middle
This is so much better than
where we were even a year ago.

21:06.966 --> 21:07.676 align:middle
It's a lot better.

21:08.106 --> 21:09.556 align:middle
Now, the job is not done.

21:09.556 --> 21:10.936 align:middle
I want to stress that,
and we're committed

21:10.936 --> 21:13.706 align:middle
to getting the job-inflation
sustainably under 2 percent,

21:13.706 --> 21:15.496 align:middle
but we need to take
note of that progress,

21:15.996 --> 21:18.326 align:middle
and we need to weigh the
risks to the labor market

21:18.326 --> 21:21.476 align:middle
and the risks to our inflation
target now more equally

21:21.476 --> 21:22.776 align:middle
than we did a year ago.

21:23.896 --> 21:24.266 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:24.876 --> 21:25.446 align:middle
Michael McKee.

21:26.966 --> 21:27.306 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

21:27.656 --> 21:29.606 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

21:30.066 --> 21:31.796 align:middle
I'd like to ask you about
the balance of risks

21:32.146 --> 21:33.826 align:middle
as the American people see it.

21:34.006 --> 21:38.236 align:middle
At this point, is the risk
greater to leave interest rates

21:38.236 --> 21:42.366 align:middle
where they are, given the damage
that higher interest rates do

21:42.426 --> 21:46.076 align:middle
to the economy in slowing
demand and raising prices?

21:46.446 --> 21:49.386 align:middle
Or is it more important
for the American people

21:49.386 --> 21:52.046 align:middle
that you keep rates where they
are to bring inflation down?

21:52.116 --> 21:52.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:53.696 --> 21:55.156 align:middle
I, I-you know, I think

21:55.156 --> 21:58.376 align:middle
that we've been given an
assignment by Congress.

21:58.686 --> 22:00.726 align:middle
This is how we serve
the American people is

22:00.776 --> 22:04.746 align:middle
by achieving maximum employment
and price stability, right?

22:04.986 --> 22:09.266 align:middle
And so in our, you know,
quasi-constitutional document,

22:09.476 --> 22:10.736 align:middle
the Statement on
Longer-Run Goals

22:10.736 --> 22:13.856 align:middle
and Monetary Policy Strategy,
we look at the two goals,

22:13.856 --> 22:15.316 align:middle
and if one of them
is farther away

22:15.316 --> 22:18.336 align:middle
than the other-the two
variables, inflation

22:18.686 --> 22:21.096 align:middle
and employment-if one is
farther away from its goal

22:21.096 --> 22:22.706 align:middle
than the other, then
you, you concentrate

22:22.706 --> 22:23.866 align:middle
on the one that's farther away.

22:24.146 --> 22:26.046 align:middle
And you take account of
the time to reach the goal.

22:26.786 --> 22:29.016 align:middle
So, for the last
couple of years,

22:29.016 --> 22:31.246 align:middle
the best service we could do
to the American people was

22:31.246 --> 22:32.336 align:middle
to focus on inflation.

22:32.866 --> 22:35.656 align:middle
But as inflation has come
down-and I think the upside

22:35.836 --> 22:37.676 align:middle
risks to inflation
have decreased

22:37.676 --> 22:40.176 align:middle
as the labor market
has cooled off,

22:40.736 --> 22:44.866 align:middle
and now the labor market
has softened-you know,

22:45.216 --> 22:47.116 align:middle
probably the inflation,

22:47.216 --> 22:49.156 align:middle
inflation's probably a little
farther from its target

22:49.186 --> 22:51.766 align:middle
than is employment, but I
think the downside risks to,

22:52.196 --> 22:56.316 align:middle
to the employment
mandate are, are real now.

22:56.316 --> 22:59.886 align:middle
So we have to weigh all that,
and if you think about where

22:59.886 --> 23:02.376 align:middle
that takes us is we have
a restrictive policy rate.

23:02.376 --> 23:03.486 align:middle
It's clearly restrictive.

23:04.016 --> 23:06.446 align:middle
It's been the rate we've had
in place for a full year.

23:07.056 --> 23:10.166 align:middle
And the time is coming-as
other central banks

23:10.166 --> 23:13.416 align:middle
around the world are facing the
same question-the time is coming

23:13.416 --> 23:16.206 align:middle
at which it will begin to
be appropriate to dial back

23:16.536 --> 23:17.746 align:middle
that level of restrictions,

23:18.506 --> 23:21.256 align:middle
restriction so that we
may address both mandates.

23:21.766 --> 23:22.246 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

23:22.286 --> 23:25.236 align:middle
Well, you have event risk
basically, with the jobs report

23:25.236 --> 23:27.936 align:middle
on Friday and another one
before you meet again.

23:28.536 --> 23:32.886 align:middle
Are you certain that you won't
fall behind the curve and lead

23:32.966 --> 23:37.916 align:middle
to unnecessary unemployment
if you wait until September?

23:37.916 --> 23:38.246 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:38.616 --> 23:42.306 align:middle
"Certainty" is not a word that
we have in our-in our business.

23:42.306 --> 23:46.306 align:middle
So, you know, we get-we get
a lot of data between now

23:46.306 --> 23:47.496 align:middle
and September, and
it isn't going

23:47.496 --> 23:49.666 align:middle
to be one data read or even two.

23:49.666 --> 23:52.696 align:middle
It's going to be the totality
of the data, all of the data,

23:52.696 --> 23:56.186 align:middle
and not just-and then how is
that affecting the outlook?

23:56.186 --> 23:57.836 align:middle
And how is it affecting
the balance of risks?

23:58.146 --> 23:59.686 align:middle
That's going to be the
assessment that we do.

24:00.046 --> 24:02.156 align:middle
Of course, we'll, we'll
all look carefully

24:02.156 --> 24:04.946 align:middle
at the employment report, but
so much other data coming in

24:05.486 --> 24:08.276 align:middle
and so much happening between
now and the September meeting,

24:08.276 --> 24:09.446 align:middle
and we'll, you know,
we'll make a judgment.

24:11.146 --> 24:12.606 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:12.606 --> 24:13.646 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

24:13.646 --> 24:14.436 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

24:14.436 --> 24:16.026 align:middle
Edward Lawrence of Fox Business.

24:16.026 --> 24:17.546 align:middle
I do want to dig deeper
on what Michael and,

24:17.546 --> 24:19.416 align:middle
and what Nick were asking.

24:19.416 --> 24:21.476 align:middle
There's a, a shift in
the statement to balance

24:21.516 --> 24:23.466 align:middle
between the focus between
inflation and jobs.

24:23.866 --> 24:24.866 align:middle
Looking at the job sides,

24:24.866 --> 24:27.656 align:middle
we've seen wage data show
sort of an abrupt slowing.

24:28.036 --> 24:30.346 align:middle
We've-we're hearing on
earnings calls from companies

24:30.346 --> 24:32.136 align:middle
like Intel abrupt layoffs.

24:32.326 --> 24:33.686 align:middle
In the jobs report from the BLS,

24:33.876 --> 24:35.986 align:middle
government jobs has
been a leading creator.

24:36.286 --> 24:39.936 align:middle
Could the government jobs-as a
sector-hiring mask underlying

24:39.936 --> 24:41.576 align:middle
weakness in the jobs report?

24:41.576 --> 24:42.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:42.706 --> 24:43.896 align:middle
Well, you know, we'll
look at everything.

24:44.986 --> 24:50.256 align:middle
We've seen some, some tendency
to have a narrowing base

24:50.256 --> 24:52.586 align:middle
of job creation in
some months going back,

24:52.586 --> 24:53.896 align:middle
but then we've had
some months where,

24:53.896 --> 24:55.936 align:middle
where job creation was broader.

24:56.326 --> 25:00.136 align:middle
And also, you know, the headline
number of jobs has come down,

25:00.136 --> 25:03.726 align:middle
so-but you look at
the whole thing.

25:03.756 --> 25:08.096 align:middle
And I think you do look at
private demand extra carefully,

25:08.206 --> 25:09.606 align:middle
to your point about,
about government.

25:10.506 --> 25:12.976 align:middle
So we'll just be looking
at, at all those things.

25:12.976 --> 25:13.706 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

25:13.706 --> 25:15.666 align:middle
Just as a follow then-so
could the Fed then be behind

25:15.666 --> 25:15.886 align:middle
the curve?

25:15.886 --> 25:17.666 align:middle
Because you said some of the
reports-in the last meeting,

25:17.666 --> 25:20.206 align:middle
you said the reports could
be noisy or overstated.

25:20.616 --> 25:23.156 align:middle
Was there a discussion
of, of-what kind

25:23.156 --> 25:24.706 align:middle
of discussion was
there for a cut today,

25:24.976 --> 25:26.846 align:middle
and could the Fed be
"behind the curve"?

25:26.896 --> 25:27.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:28.276 --> 25:33.826 align:middle
Yeah, so look, the objective is
to balance the two risks, right?

25:33.826 --> 25:36.656 align:middle
It's the risk of going too soon
and the risk of going too late.

25:37.106 --> 25:40.416 align:middle
We've been-you know,
we, we had seven months

25:40.476 --> 25:43.296 align:middle
of good inflation data
at the end of last year.

25:43.566 --> 25:44.746 align:middle
We said we wanted to see more.

25:44.746 --> 25:47.916 align:middle
We said-we pointed out that
too much of this was coming

25:47.916 --> 25:50.796 align:middle
from goods, and, sure enough,
the first quarter wasn't,

25:50.916 --> 25:52.086 align:middle
wasn't great-inflation data.

25:52.086 --> 25:54.806 align:middle
And now we've got another
quarter-a quarter that is good.

25:55.386 --> 25:58.676 align:middle
And, you know, we're balancing
the risk of going too soon

25:58.676 --> 25:59.896 align:middle
against the risk
of going too late.

25:59.896 --> 26:00.646 align:middle
That's what we're doing.

26:00.786 --> 26:02.496 align:middle
There's no guarantee in this.

26:02.496 --> 26:04.076 align:middle
It's a very difficult
judgment call,

26:04.076 --> 26:08.036 align:middle
but this is-this is
how we're making it.

26:08.036 --> 26:12.476 align:middle
So-but in terms of today,
your question about today,

26:12.896 --> 26:18.646 align:middle
we did have a-you know, we
had a nice conversation about,

26:18.836 --> 26:20.456 align:middle
about this issue today.

26:20.596 --> 26:23.016 align:middle
The overall sense of the
Committee, as I mentioned,

26:23.016 --> 26:24.346 align:middle
is that we're getting
closer to the point

26:24.666 --> 26:26.106 align:middle
at which it will be
appropriate to begin

26:26.366 --> 26:27.526 align:middle
to dial back restriction,

26:27.596 --> 26:29.456 align:middle
but we're not quite
at that point yet.

26:29.716 --> 26:30.926 align:middle
We want to see more good data.

26:31.666 --> 26:35.736 align:middle
The decision was unanimous-all
19 participants supported it.

26:36.336 --> 26:39.586 align:middle
But there was a real
discussion back and forth

26:39.786 --> 26:44.346 align:middle
of what the case would be for,
for moving at this meeting.

26:44.586 --> 26:48.596 align:middle
A strong majority
supported moving-not moving

26:48.596 --> 26:49.046 align:middle
at this meeting.

26:49.046 --> 26:50.446 align:middle
That was the strong
sense of the Committee.

26:50.446 --> 26:52.966 align:middle
But it's a conversation that
we had today, certainly.

26:53.716 --> 26:54.266 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

26:54.876 --> 26:55.286 align:middle
Courtenay.

26:55.826 --> 26:57.656 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

26:57.896 --> 26:59.316 align:middle
Courtenay Brown from Axios.

26:59.366 --> 27:00.866 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

27:01.346 --> 27:04.456 align:middle
When the Fed was raising rates,
there was a lot of conversation

27:04.506 --> 27:07.356 align:middle
about long and variable lags.

27:07.356 --> 27:10.886 align:middle
I wonder if that applies
on the way down, too.

27:10.886 --> 27:12.726 align:middle
How are you and the
Committee thinking about that?

27:13.446 --> 27:13.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:13.686 --> 27:14.376 align:middle
Yes, it does.

27:14.886 --> 27:17.096 align:middle
And I think the lags have
kind of showed up here

27:17.096 --> 27:18.346 align:middle
in the last six months,
by the way.

27:18.946 --> 27:21.756 align:middle
You, you really do now see the
restriction, whereas, I mean,

27:21.816 --> 27:23.166 align:middle
even a few months ago,

27:23.166 --> 27:25.676 align:middle
people were questioning
how restrictive policy was.

27:25.676 --> 27:27.346 align:middle
Look at the labor
market now-you can see.

27:27.766 --> 27:31.316 align:middle
And look at
inflation-sorry-rate-sensitive,

27:31.696 --> 27:32.726 align:middle
interest-sensitive spending.

27:32.726 --> 27:35.026 align:middle
You really do see now that
policy is restrictive.

27:35.026 --> 27:37.066 align:middle
I wouldn't say it's
extremely restrictive,

27:37.066 --> 27:38.766 align:middle
but it's certainly
effectively restrictive.

27:39.176 --> 27:42.896 align:middle
Yes, there-the lag should,
should be on the way down.

27:42.966 --> 27:46.096 align:middle
It should take some
time to get into-to get

27:46.096 --> 27:48.116 align:middle
into the full economy,
affect financial conditions.

27:48.116 --> 27:51.766 align:middle
And that affects economic
activity, hiring and that kind

27:51.766 --> 27:52.846 align:middle
of thing, and, ultimately,
inflation.

27:52.846 --> 27:54.206 align:middle
It doesn't-it's not
instantaneous,

27:54.526 --> 27:55.926 align:middle
although it's faster
than it used to be

27:55.926 --> 27:59.206 align:middle
because markets move now in
anticipation of our moves.

27:59.206 --> 27:59.506 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

27:59.956 --> 28:03.976 align:middle
So are you worried then that if
monetary policy acts with long

28:03.976 --> 28:06.686 align:middle
and variable lags, even when
you're lowering interest rates,

28:07.306 --> 28:11.556 align:middle
it might be too late for the
Fed to help stave off any kind

28:11.556 --> 28:14.576 align:middle
of slowdown in the labor
market or broader economy?

28:14.576 --> 28:14.796 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:14.796 --> 28:15.766 align:middle
We have to worry about that.

28:15.846 --> 28:19.766 align:middle
I mean, we-just to make
it clear, you know,

28:19.766 --> 28:22.266 align:middle
it's a very difficult,
challenging judgment,

28:23.126 --> 28:25.656 align:middle
and we don't want
to go too soon,

28:25.656 --> 28:27.476 align:middle
and we don't want
to go too late.

28:28.196 --> 28:30.646 align:middle
But that's-this is how
we made that judgment.

28:30.926 --> 28:32.236 align:middle
I feel good about where we are.

28:32.776 --> 28:35.306 align:middle
We're certainly very well
positioned to respond

28:35.306 --> 28:38.176 align:middle
to weakness with the
policy rate at 5.3 percent.

28:38.666 --> 28:40.316 align:middle
We certainly have a
lot of room to respond

28:40.316 --> 28:41.496 align:middle
if we were to see weakness.

28:42.226 --> 28:43.316 align:middle
That's not what we're
seeing, though.

28:43.316 --> 28:44.436 align:middle
What we're seeing-look
at the-look

28:44.436 --> 28:45.706 align:middle
at the first-half
growth numbers.

28:45.706 --> 28:48.426 align:middle
Look at PDFP at 2.6
percent for the first half.

28:48.426 --> 28:50.606 align:middle
It's not signaling
a weak economy.

28:50.916 --> 28:54.506 align:middle
It's also not signaling
an overheating economy.

28:54.716 --> 28:57.316 align:middle
Labor market-admittedly, the
unemployment rate has moved

28:57.316 --> 29:00.656 align:middle
up seven-tenths, and
we're seeing-we're seeing

29:00.656 --> 29:02.976 align:middle
normalization there, but wage,

29:03.086 --> 29:04.916 align:middle
wage increases are
still at a high level.

29:04.916 --> 29:06.406 align:middle
Unemployment is still
at a low level.

29:06.406 --> 29:07.496 align:middle
Layoffs are very low.

29:08.116 --> 29:10.786 align:middle
Initial claims have moved up,
but they're pretty stable,

29:10.786 --> 29:12.626 align:middle
and they're historically
not high at all.

29:12.626 --> 29:16.536 align:middle
So the total scope of the data
suggests a normalizing labor

29:16.536 --> 29:20.586 align:middle
market, and, again, we are
carefully watching to see

29:20.636 --> 29:22.296 align:middle
that that continues
to be the case.

29:23.136 --> 29:23.936 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

29:23.936 --> 29:26.686 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

29:27.246 --> 29:28.676 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

29:29.026 --> 29:30.876 align:middle
On the labor market,
I was wondering,

29:30.876 --> 29:34.196 align:middle
how worried are you all about
unemployment rising to the point

29:34.196 --> 29:35.786 align:middle
where it triggers the Sahm rule,

29:36.036 --> 29:38.166 align:middle
and would that potentially
affect how quickly you

29:38.456 --> 29:38.946 align:middle
cut rates?

29:39.386 --> 29:40.846 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:43.836 --> 29:49.196 align:middle
We-so I would just say, the
question really is, is one of,

29:49.276 --> 29:51.696 align:middle
are we worried about a sharper
downturn in the labor market?

29:51.746 --> 29:52.946 align:middle
So, and the answer is,

29:52.946 --> 29:54.486 align:middle
we're watching really
carefully for that.

29:54.926 --> 29:58.846 align:middle
We're, we're aware of that rule,
which is really a, you know,

29:58.996 --> 30:04.416 align:middle
a-I would call it a
statistical, statistical thing

30:04.416 --> 30:06.626 align:middle
that has happened
through history.

30:07.456 --> 30:11.226 align:middle
A "statistical regularity"
is what I'd call it.

30:11.386 --> 30:12.786 align:middle
It's not like an economic rule

30:12.786 --> 30:14.726 align:middle
where it's telling you
something must happen.

30:15.566 --> 30:17.466 align:middle
So, again, what do we see?

30:17.516 --> 30:18.536 align:middle
What are our eyes telling us?

30:18.536 --> 30:20.546 align:middle
We look at-we look at all
the things we're seeing,

30:21.106 --> 30:24.706 align:middle
and what it looks like is a
normalizing labor market-again,

30:24.956 --> 30:28.246 align:middle
job creation at a pretty
decent level, wages moving

30:28.246 --> 30:31.146 align:middle
up at a strong level but
coming down gradually.

30:31.806 --> 30:34.546 align:middle
Job vacancies have come
down, but they're still high

30:34.796 --> 30:35.866 align:middle
by historical standards.

30:35.866 --> 30:38.936 align:middle
So, again, I've been through
some of the data already,

30:38.936 --> 30:40.696 align:middle
but what we think
we're seeing is,

30:40.866 --> 30:45.106 align:middle
is a normalizing labor market,
and we're watching carefully

30:45.106 --> 30:47.186 align:middle
to see if it-if it
turns out to be more.

30:47.186 --> 30:49.086 align:middle
If it starts to show signs
that it's more than that,

30:49.636 --> 30:51.546 align:middle
then we're well positioned
to respond.

30:52.116 --> 30:52.496 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

30:52.496 --> 30:53.156 align:middle
Is there reason to think

30:53.156 --> 30:55.096 align:middle
that the labor market might
behave differently this time

30:55.096 --> 30:56.036 align:middle
than it has historically?

30:56.256 --> 30:57.106 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:57.106 --> 31:00.976 align:middle
I think, you know, history
doesn't repeat itself;

31:01.226 --> 31:02.336 align:middle
it rhymes.

31:02.336 --> 31:04.736 align:middle
That statement is very
true about the economy.

31:04.736 --> 31:06.536 align:middle
You never assume it's
going to be just the same.

31:06.686 --> 31:09.576 align:middle
An example would be, is
there a trend increase

31:09.576 --> 31:10.686 align:middle
in the level of vacancies?

31:10.986 --> 31:13.536 align:middle
There are many, many examples,
so it's never exactly the same.

31:13.916 --> 31:17.586 align:middle
Also, let's remember that
this pandemic era has been one

31:17.586 --> 31:19.106 align:middle
in which so many, you know,

31:19.106 --> 31:20.566 align:middle
apparent rules have
been flouted,

31:20.766 --> 31:24.126 align:middle
like the inverted yield
curve for starters.

31:25.376 --> 31:27.946 align:middle
So, many, many received-pieces

31:27.946 --> 31:29.506 align:middle
of received wisdom
just haven't worked,

31:29.506 --> 31:32.846 align:middle
and it's because this situation
really is unusual or unique

31:33.436 --> 31:36.806 align:middle
in that so much of this
inflation came from the shutdown

31:36.806 --> 31:41.396 align:middle
of the economy and the resulting
supply problems in the face of,

31:41.396 --> 31:43.026 align:middle
admittedly, very strong demand.

31:43.026 --> 31:48.266 align:middle
So the whole-the whole situation
is not the same as many

31:48.266 --> 31:52.406 align:middle
of the other prior inflation
or downturns that we've seen

31:53.126 --> 31:54.406 align:middle
or business cycles
that we've seen.

31:54.406 --> 31:55.526 align:middle
So we're having to learn.

31:55.696 --> 32:00.146 align:middle
We're having to, to be very
careful about the judgments

32:00.146 --> 32:01.176 align:middle
that we make, I would say.

32:01.176 --> 32:03.726 align:middle
So we don't assume that
these regularities will,

32:03.796 --> 32:05.406 align:middle
will just repeat
themselves automatically.

32:05.406 --> 32:06.496 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

32:08.356 --> 32:10.876 align:middle
Amara. AMARA OMEOKWE.

32:11.176 --> 32:11.886 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

32:11.886 --> 32:13.276 align:middle
Amara Omeokwe with Bloomberg.

32:13.646 --> 32:15.006 align:middle
There seems to be
quite a difference

32:15.046 --> 32:17.466 align:middle
between what the anecdotal
data are telling us,

32:17.466 --> 32:21.736 align:middle
such as the very recent downbeat
Beige Book, and the hard data.

32:21.736 --> 32:23.976 align:middle
Do you take those
anecdotes seriously-that is,

32:23.976 --> 32:26.806 align:middle
that the economy and labor
market are cooling much more

32:26.806 --> 32:28.216 align:middle
rapidly than what's
shown in the data?

32:29.336 --> 32:29.616 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:29.906 --> 32:33.286 align:middle
So I do take that seriously,
and the Beige Book is great.

32:33.576 --> 32:37.716 align:middle
What's even greater is hearing
the Reserve Bank presidents come

32:37.716 --> 32:40.276 align:middle
in and talk about their
conversations with,

32:41.196 --> 32:44.886 align:middle
with businesses and business
leaders and workers and people

32:44.886 --> 32:47.496 align:middle
in the nonprofit sector
in their Districts.

32:47.526 --> 32:50.886 align:middle
But I'll tell you, it's
a pretty-the picture is,

32:51.386 --> 32:54.646 align:middle
is not one of a slowing or,
you know, a really bad economy.

32:54.646 --> 32:57.426 align:middle
It's one of there are spots of
weakness, and there are regions

32:57.486 --> 33:00.346 align:middle
where growth is stronger than
other regions, but, overall,

33:00.886 --> 33:03.176 align:middle
it's-again, look at
the aggregate data.

33:03.746 --> 33:07.176 align:middle
The aggregate data
is-particularly PDFP,

33:07.176 --> 33:10.676 align:middle
private domestic final
purchases-is 2.6 percent,

33:10.676 --> 33:14.046 align:middle
and that's a good indicator
of private-of private demand.

33:14.426 --> 33:16.096 align:middle
So we listen to all
of that, and it does,

33:16.166 --> 33:20.236 align:middle
does-I think it's important
to listen to anecdotal data

33:20.236 --> 33:21.706 align:middle
and not just look at
the aggregate data.

33:21.706 --> 33:26.016 align:middle
Especially, you know, it's very
hard-GDP data can be volatile

33:26.196 --> 33:28.076 align:middle
quarter to quarter.

33:28.456 --> 33:31.646 align:middle
So it's just hard to
measure economic activity.

33:31.646 --> 33:33.676 align:middle
There are a lot of-it's
just difficult to do.

33:34.246 --> 33:36.686 align:middle
So I look at both,
but I wouldn't say

33:36.686 --> 33:40.286 align:middle
that the-that the anecdotal
data is uniformly downbeat.

33:40.356 --> 33:41.356 align:middle
It's more mixed.

33:43.606 --> 33:46.356 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:46.986 --> 33:48.036 align:middle
Jo Ling. JO LING KENT.

33:48.486 --> 33:49.156 align:middle
Thank you.

33:49.156 --> 33:50.706 align:middle
Jo Ling Kent with CBS News.

33:50.856 --> 33:52.746 align:middle
Chair Powell, thanks for
taking our questions today.

33:53.066 --> 33:54.216 align:middle
You have consistently said

33:54.216 --> 33:57.056 align:middle
that the Fed does not consider
politics in making decisions.

33:57.346 --> 33:59.546 align:middle
With a possible September
rate cut on the table,

34:00.196 --> 34:02.376 align:middle
it would be less than two
months before the election,

34:02.376 --> 34:04.356 align:middle
and former President
Trump reportedly said

34:04.356 --> 34:05.686 align:middle
that cutting rates so close

34:05.686 --> 34:08.426 align:middle
to the election is something
the central bank knows

34:08.486 --> 34:09.606 align:middle
"they shouldn't be doing."

34:09.976 --> 34:12.676 align:middle
What's your response, and
do you believe it's possible

34:12.746 --> 34:15.496 align:middle
to really remain apolitical
with a September rate cut?

34:16.166 --> 34:17.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:17.266 --> 34:19.266 align:middle
I absolutely do, and I
think it's-first of all,

34:19.266 --> 34:21.176 align:middle
we haven't made any decisions.

34:21.346 --> 34:23.596 align:middle
I would say it this way:
haven't made any decision

34:23.596 --> 34:24.706 align:middle
about any future meeting.

34:25.156 --> 34:27.646 align:middle
I don't know what the
data will reveal or how

34:27.646 --> 34:30.266 align:middle
that will affect the
appropriate path of our policy.

34:30.266 --> 34:30.976 align:middle
I really don't know.

34:31.356 --> 34:33.146 align:middle
I do know how we will
make that assessment.

34:33.256 --> 34:33.976 align:middle
That's what I do know.

34:34.756 --> 34:37.886 align:middle
So if you take a step back,
the current situation, again,

34:37.886 --> 34:41.016 align:middle
is inflation has come down
much closer to our goal,

34:41.576 --> 34:44.286 align:middle
and that's happened while
unemployment has remained low.

34:44.376 --> 34:47.316 align:middle
We're, we're very tightly
focused on using our tools

34:47.836 --> 34:51.736 align:middle
to try to foster that state
of affairs continuing.

34:52.436 --> 34:54.476 align:middle
That's at each of our meetings
and all of our decisions,

34:54.476 --> 34:56.656 align:middle
our focus is strictly
on that and really

34:56.656 --> 35:01.146 align:middle
on nothing else-doing our part,
whatever that part may be.

35:01.706 --> 35:03.626 align:middle
You know, we're using
our best thinking.

35:03.696 --> 35:07.016 align:middle
We're doing our best to
understand the economy.

35:07.016 --> 35:08.946 align:middle
We, we follow academics.

35:08.946 --> 35:11.306 align:middle
We follow the many
commentators who bless us

35:11.306 --> 35:12.836 align:middle
with their commentary
[laughter],

35:13.696 --> 35:17.286 align:middle
but we don't change
anything in our approach

35:18.106 --> 35:20.356 align:middle
to address other factors,
like the political calendar.

35:20.396 --> 35:21.756 align:middle
Congress has, we believe,

35:21.756 --> 35:25.246 align:middle
ordered us to conduct our
business in a nonpolitical way

35:25.846 --> 35:27.636 align:middle
at all times, not
just some of the time.

35:28.566 --> 35:32.316 align:middle
I'll say this, too: We never
use our tools to support

35:32.316 --> 35:34.406 align:middle
or oppose a political
party, a politician,

35:34.406 --> 35:35.676 align:middle
or any political outcome.

35:36.886 --> 35:40.936 align:middle
The bottom line is, if we do
our very best to do our part

35:40.936 --> 35:43.996 align:middle
and we stick to our part, that
will benefit all Americans.

35:44.556 --> 35:47.126 align:middle
If we get it right, the
economy will be stronger.

35:47.466 --> 35:48.566 align:middle
We'll have price stability.

35:48.736 --> 35:49.946 align:middle
People will find jobs.

35:50.206 --> 35:51.836 align:middle
Wages will rise in real terms.

35:52.486 --> 35:53.496 align:middle
Everyone will benefit.

35:54.166 --> 35:57.336 align:middle
So that's what we believe, and
that's how we will always act.

35:57.336 --> 35:59.846 align:middle
This is my fourth presidential
election at the Fed.

36:00.316 --> 36:02.136 align:middle
I can tell you this is
how we think about it.

36:02.706 --> 36:03.616 align:middle
This is what we do.

36:04.056 --> 36:07.336 align:middle
So anything that we
do before, during,

36:07.336 --> 36:11.066 align:middle
or after the election will be
based on the data, the outlook,

36:11.726 --> 36:14.816 align:middle
and the balance of risks
and not on anything else.

36:14.816 --> 36:15.566 align:middle
JO LING KENT.

36:15.566 --> 36:18.106 align:middle
Just a quick follow-up-do
your economic forecasts

36:18.376 --> 36:22.366 align:middle
and models take into account
the two very different economic

36:22.426 --> 36:24.946 align:middle
plans of these two
presidential candidates,

36:24.946 --> 36:27.056 align:middle
Harris and Trump,
and, if so, how?

36:27.566 --> 36:27.876 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:27.876 --> 36:29.396 align:middle
No. We do not do that.

36:29.876 --> 36:31.216 align:middle
We absolutely do not do that.

36:31.216 --> 36:32.666 align:middle
We don't-we don't know
who's going to win.

36:33.056 --> 36:34.126 align:middle
We don't know what
they're going to do.

36:34.126 --> 36:35.786 align:middle
We don't act as though we know,

36:35.786 --> 36:37.756 align:middle
and we just can't
do that, you know?

36:37.756 --> 36:40.086 align:middle
We basically-we have
our forecast.

36:40.086 --> 36:45.136 align:middle
We're not-we can run simulations
of different potential policies,

36:45.136 --> 36:48.616 align:middle
but we would never try to
make policy decisions based

36:48.616 --> 36:50.956 align:middle
on the outcome of an election
that hasn't happened yet.

36:50.956 --> 36:53.816 align:middle
We would just-that would just
be a line we would never cross.

36:54.316 --> 36:55.946 align:middle
You know, we're a
nonpolitical agency.

36:55.946 --> 37:00.006 align:middle
We don't want to be involved
in any-in politics in any way,

37:00.006 --> 37:00.776 align:middle
so we wouldn't do that.

37:00.776 --> 37:01.046 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:01.306 --> 37:05.916 align:middle
Nicholas. NICHOLAS JASINSKI.

37:05.916 --> 37:06.646 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

37:06.646 --> 37:08.396 align:middle
Nicholas Jasinski from
Barron's Magazine.

37:08.986 --> 37:11.006 align:middle
There hasn't been
a dissenting vote

37:11.006 --> 37:13.186 align:middle
on an interest rate
decision in some time.

37:13.186 --> 37:15.906 align:middle
If the data do evolve
as you expect,

37:15.906 --> 37:18.426 align:middle
if you do have more confidence
by the September meeting,

37:18.886 --> 37:21.096 align:middle
do you get the sense that
there will be a unanimous vote

37:21.186 --> 37:23.506 align:middle
on an interest rate
move in September?

37:23.756 --> 37:25.656 align:middle
Basically, are there
meaningful differences

37:25.656 --> 37:28.266 align:middle
in Committee members'
assessments

37:28.266 --> 37:30.036 align:middle
of how much more
confidence is needed?

37:30.546 --> 37:31.496 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:31.496 --> 37:33.906 align:middle
So there's-there are always
meaningful differences.

37:33.906 --> 37:34.436 align:middle
There are.

37:34.536 --> 37:38.366 align:middle
And, you know, we talk
a lot before, during,

37:38.366 --> 37:39.196 align:middle
and after the meeting.

37:39.196 --> 37:42.986 align:middle
We do have a very robust
discussion of these things.

37:43.456 --> 37:47.706 align:middle
You're right that in,
in most cases, people,

37:47.706 --> 37:49.196 align:middle
if they feel heard and they feel

37:49.196 --> 37:52.846 align:middle
that they've-that their
position has been given serious

37:52.846 --> 37:54.876 align:middle
consideration, for most
people, most of the time,

37:55.336 --> 37:56.126 align:middle
that's going to be enough.

37:56.536 --> 37:57.316 align:middle
There are dissents.

37:57.496 --> 37:58.146 align:middle
That's fine.

37:58.896 --> 38:00.246 align:middle
You know, no one has a veto.

38:00.826 --> 38:04.656 align:middle
No single person has a veto,
so it just is a question of,

38:05.376 --> 38:06.806 align:middle
who will vote for and against?

38:06.806 --> 38:10.966 align:middle
We've had-we've had,
you know, dissents.

38:11.396 --> 38:14.596 align:middle
We haven't had so many
during the pandemic era,

38:14.596 --> 38:19.666 align:middle
and it just may be that we, we
felt more united because we felt

38:19.666 --> 38:21.436 align:middle
under a lot of pressure
to get things right,

38:21.646 --> 38:24.556 align:middle
but before the pandemic,
we had plenty of dissents.

38:24.866 --> 38:27.026 align:middle
And dissents happen.

38:27.516 --> 38:28.946 align:middle
It's part of the process.

38:29.216 --> 38:30.696 align:middle
There's nothing wrong
with dissents,

38:31.456 --> 38:32.866 align:middle
and if it happens, it happens.

38:34.416 --> 38:35.556 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:35.596 --> 38:38.066 align:middle
Jean. JEAN YUNG.

38:38.326 --> 38:40.136 align:middle
Hello. Jean Yung
with MNI Market News.

38:40.136 --> 38:45.036 align:middle
Is a 50 basis point
cut as a first cut

38:45.036 --> 38:46.676 align:middle
at all likely or
even on the table?

38:47.226 --> 38:47.496 align:middle
Thank you.

38:47.666 --> 38:48.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:48.276 --> 38:51.106 align:middle
You know, I don't want to say-I
don't want to be really specific

38:51.106 --> 38:52.266 align:middle
about what we're going
to do, but that's,

38:52.266 --> 38:54.466 align:middle
that's not something we're
thinking about right now.

38:55.406 --> 38:55.686 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:57.106 --> 38:57.986 align:middle
Jennifer. CHAIR POWELL.

38:57.986 --> 39:00.766 align:middle
Of course, we haven't made any
decisions at all as of today.

39:00.766 --> 39:02.156 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

39:03.076 --> 39:03.766 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

39:03.766 --> 39:05.646 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger
with Yahoo Finance.

39:05.886 --> 39:06.916 align:middle
Not to get into the minutes,

39:06.966 --> 39:08.796 align:middle
but you said there was
a real discussion today

39:08.796 --> 39:10.256 align:middle
for moving at this meeting.

39:10.256 --> 39:13.406 align:middle
I'm curious if you could provide
some more color on the nature

39:13.406 --> 39:15.106 align:middle
of the discussion
today at the meeting

39:15.216 --> 39:17.886 align:middle
about a possible rate cut
as early as September.

39:17.886 --> 39:21.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:21.366 --> 39:26.576 align:middle
Well, so, you know, the
way the meeting is set up,

39:26.906 --> 39:28.446 align:middle
the first day there's
a discussion

39:28.446 --> 39:30.976 align:middle
of financial stability because
it's every other meeting we have

39:31.016 --> 39:33.176 align:middle
that, and then we have an
opportunity to comment on that.

39:33.526 --> 39:35.286 align:middle
Then we have an economic
go-round.

39:35.936 --> 39:40.586 align:middle
And then, this morning, we have
the monetary policy go-round.

39:40.586 --> 39:42.846 align:middle
And I think in people's economic

39:42.846 --> 39:44.956 align:middle
or in their monetary
policy go-round,

39:45.496 --> 39:48.946 align:middle
people express their views
about this, and, you know,

39:48.946 --> 39:50.026 align:middle
there's a range of views.

39:50.026 --> 39:51.736 align:middle
People-as you will
know from the speeches

39:51.736 --> 39:53.576 align:middle
that they give-people
have different ways

39:53.576 --> 39:54.756 align:middle
of thinking about the economy.

39:54.756 --> 39:58.296 align:middle
And so, in the minutes,
we'll lay this

39:58.296 --> 40:01.826 align:middle
out in a much-in a much better
way than I can do off the cuff,

40:02.476 --> 40:04.316 align:middle
but there's a range
of perspectives.

40:04.556 --> 40:07.256 align:middle
And, you know-but I
do think that we are,

40:07.256 --> 40:09.166 align:middle
we're a consensus-driven
organization.

40:09.166 --> 40:09.936 align:middle
People come together.

40:09.936 --> 40:12.686 align:middle
This was a unanimous-a
unanimous decision.

40:12.686 --> 40:14.556 align:middle
And, at the end, everyone,

40:14.796 --> 40:17.666 align:middle
everyone supported the
outcome-not just the voters,

40:17.666 --> 40:18.186 align:middle
but everyone.

40:18.766 --> 40:23.566 align:middle
So I would also say, some
people examined the possibility,

40:23.736 --> 40:28.436 align:middle
you know, the case for
moving at this meeting, but,

40:29.096 --> 40:31.936 align:middle
overwhelmingly, the sense
of the Committee was not

40:31.936 --> 40:34.066 align:middle
at this meeting but as
soon as the next meeting,

40:34.066 --> 40:35.376 align:middle
depending on how
the data come in.

40:35.376 --> 40:35.766 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

40:35.906 --> 40:38.506 align:middle
But there is a growing sense of
confidence that you could move

40:38.506 --> 40:39.366 align:middle
at the next meeting-
CHAIR POWELL.

40:39.366 --> 40:39.976 align:middle
Yes. JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

40:39.976 --> 40:40.836 align:middle
-assuming inflation comes?

40:41.256 --> 40:41.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:41.936 --> 40:43.276 align:middle
Well, assuming that the totality

40:43.276 --> 40:44.716 align:middle
of the data supports
such an outcome.

40:44.716 --> 40:45.416 align:middle
No question.

40:45.536 --> 40:54.636 align:middle
That's, that is the
case, as I mentioned.

40:54.636 --> 40:59.806 align:middle
We think that the time
is, is-it's approaching,

41:00.706 --> 41:03.846 align:middle
and if we do get the data
that we-that we hope we get,

41:04.116 --> 41:07.706 align:middle
then a reduction in our policy
rate could be on the table

41:07.706 --> 41:08.436 align:middle
at the September meeting.

41:08.466 --> 41:09.786 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:09.786 --> 41:13.776 align:middle
Nancy. NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

41:14.916 --> 41:15.876 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

41:15.876 --> 41:17.856 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

41:18.426 --> 41:21.956 align:middle
Former New York president
Bill Dudley wrote an op-ed

41:21.956 --> 41:25.106 align:middle
in Bloomberg earlier this
month, which you probably saw,

41:25.106 --> 41:28.236 align:middle
in which he said, "It
might already be too late

41:28.236 --> 41:30.516 align:middle
to fend off a recession
by cutting rates,

41:30.516 --> 41:34.116 align:middle
dawdling now unnecessarily
increases the risk."

41:34.436 --> 41:35.006 align:middle
Is he wrong?

41:35.576 --> 41:36.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:36.506 --> 41:39.016 align:middle
So this is the judgment that
we have to make, and we're,

41:39.066 --> 41:41.106 align:middle
we're well aware
of the judgment.

41:41.296 --> 41:45.816 align:middle
We're-as I've said, we have to
weigh the risk of going too soon

41:46.436 --> 41:48.386 align:middle
against the risk
of going too late.

41:48.386 --> 41:53.536 align:middle
If we go too soon, we can-we
had a lot of advice, you know,

41:53.536 --> 41:57.026 align:middle
to go ahead and cut after the
seven good months of last year.

41:57.026 --> 41:57.466 align:middle
We didn't.

41:57.466 --> 41:58.646 align:middle
We said we needed to see more.

41:59.166 --> 42:00.506 align:middle
Then we saw some
higher inflation.

42:00.806 --> 42:02.476 align:middle
We've seen one quarter
of good inflation,

42:02.576 --> 42:05.356 align:middle
and we've seen the labor
market move quite a bit.

42:05.666 --> 42:08.016 align:middle
And, as I mentioned,
I don't think it needs

42:08.016 --> 42:13.736 align:middle
to cool off anymore for us
to get the inflation results

42:13.736 --> 42:15.976 align:middle
that are related to the labor
market-not all inflation is,

42:15.976 --> 42:16.426 align:middle
of course.

42:17.056 --> 42:19.846 align:middle
So I think it's a
difficult judgment to make,

42:19.846 --> 42:22.096 align:middle
and what you see is the
judgment of the Committee is

42:22.166 --> 42:24.866 align:middle
that that time is drawing near.

42:24.866 --> 42:29.276 align:middle
That time could be in September
if, if the data support that.

42:29.276 --> 42:30.966 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

42:30.966 --> 42:34.686 align:middle
And have the chances of
a hard landing increased?

42:34.876 --> 42:35.466 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:36.296 --> 42:38.856 align:middle
So I, I don't know
whether they've increased.

42:38.856 --> 42:39.736 align:middle
I think they're low.

42:39.866 --> 42:44.316 align:middle
I think this is-you don't
see any reason to think

42:44.346 --> 42:46.146 align:middle
that this economy is
either overheating

42:46.986 --> 42:49.186 align:middle
or sharply weakening.

42:49.366 --> 42:51.966 align:middle
That's just not in
the data right now.

42:51.996 --> 42:54.446 align:middle
What's in the data right now
is an economy that's growing

42:54.446 --> 42:59.616 align:middle
at a-at a solid pace, a labor
market that has cooled off, but,

42:59.856 --> 43:02.836 align:middle
nonetheless, inflation-sorry,
unemployment is low.

43:02.836 --> 43:05.946 align:middle
The data overall show
a strong labor market.

43:06.726 --> 43:10.926 align:middle
And so that's, that's
really what you see.

43:11.206 --> 43:13.216 align:middle
It's not-it's neither
an overheating economy,

43:13.216 --> 43:16.116 align:middle
nor is it a sharply
weakening economy.

43:16.546 --> 43:19.076 align:middle
It's, it's kind of what
you would want to see, but,

43:19.076 --> 43:21.356 align:middle
of course, the job
is never done.

43:21.356 --> 43:24.826 align:middle
You know, we're, we're
watching to see, you know,

43:24.976 --> 43:26.206 align:middle
which way the economy heads.

43:26.206 --> 43:29.326 align:middle
And I think we're-if we
are to respond to weakness,

43:29.326 --> 43:32.056 align:middle
we're certainly, you know,
well equipped to do that.

43:32.346 --> 43:33.716 align:middle
But that's not what
we're seeing.

43:33.716 --> 43:37.446 align:middle
What we're seeing is strong
economic activity and, you know,

43:37.446 --> 43:39.216 align:middle
a good labor market and
inflation coming down.

43:40.856 --> 43:42.266 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

43:44.136 --> 43:44.896 align:middle
Greg. GREG ROBB.

43:45.186 --> 43:45.956 align:middle
Thank you so much.

43:47.296 --> 43:50.686 align:middle
In the-in the minutes of
the June meeting that came

43:50.686 --> 43:54.896 align:middle
out a few weeks ago, there was a
discussion about communications,

43:54.896 --> 43:57.916 align:middle
and some Fed officials
said maybe the Fed wasn't

43:57.916 --> 44:00.686 align:middle
as clear enough about
its reaction function.

44:01.216 --> 44:04.596 align:middle
And, and when I talked to the
commentators who bless you

44:04.596 --> 44:05.556 align:middle
with their comments [laughter],

44:05.766 --> 44:10.906 align:middle
they say that they really don't
have a sense of what is going

44:10.906 --> 44:13.876 align:middle
to judge, maybe not the
first cut, but the pace

44:13.876 --> 44:15.236 align:middle
of the cuts going forward.

44:15.236 --> 44:16.796 align:middle
They don't have a
good sense of that.

44:16.796 --> 44:19.516 align:middle
Is there anything you can say,
like how will we judge that?

44:19.866 --> 44:20.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:20.436 --> 44:25.876 align:middle
Yeah, I mean, I think
the reality is that,

44:26.336 --> 44:28.886 align:middle
that forecasters-and
this isn't just the Fed

44:29.136 --> 44:32.736 align:middle
by any means-forecasters have
been continually surprised by,

44:33.066 --> 44:35.956 align:middle
for example, the strength
of the economy last year.

44:36.716 --> 44:39.196 align:middle
So I think we had to
be pretty humble about,

44:39.836 --> 44:41.826 align:middle
about giving forward
guidance about this,

44:41.876 --> 44:42.686 align:middle
that, and the other thing.

44:42.686 --> 44:44.476 align:middle
We need to be pretty
careful about that.

44:44.476 --> 44:46.166 align:middle
And, you know, when
you're saying you're going

44:46.166 --> 44:47.606 align:middle
to be data driven, of course,

44:47.606 --> 44:50.316 align:middle
it's always what the data-how
they affect the outlook

44:50.316 --> 44:51.346 align:middle
and the balance of risks,

44:52.246 --> 44:55.816 align:middle
but it's-nobody has great
vision deep into the future.

44:56.486 --> 44:57.836 align:middle
In terms of a reaction function,

44:57.836 --> 45:00.326 align:middle
that's a-that's a
long-time discussion

45:00.326 --> 45:02.116 align:middle
that people have had forever.

45:02.836 --> 45:06.676 align:middle
I think people have understood
for a long time, actually,

45:06.676 --> 45:08.936 align:middle
that we were very focused
on bringing down inflation.

45:08.996 --> 45:10.626 align:middle
Nobody was really
confused about that.

45:11.286 --> 45:14.716 align:middle
The data have, you know-again,

45:15.076 --> 45:16.926 align:middle
we've seen significant
improvement

45:16.926 --> 45:18.566 align:middle
in inflation just
for the last quarter.

45:18.996 --> 45:21.306 align:middle
Markets move around on
that-on the data, really.

45:21.306 --> 45:23.746 align:middle
Not so much-it's not really
what we're going to do.

45:23.746 --> 45:26.496 align:middle
It's more just that the
data keep coming in,

45:26.536 --> 45:28.486 align:middle
and markets are very,
very responsive

45:28.486 --> 45:29.436 align:middle
to that data right now.

45:31.076 --> 45:31.406 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

45:31.956 --> 45:33.856 align:middle
Go to Jeff for the
last question.

45:34.366 --> 45:36.016 align:middle
JEFF COX. Thank you,
Mr. Chairman.

45:36.276 --> 45:39.066 align:middle
I'm going to change gears on
you just a little bit from all

45:39.066 --> 45:40.626 align:middle
of the rate talk and whatnot.

45:41.276 --> 45:45.996 align:middle
With FedNow being in the books
for a little over a year,

45:46.276 --> 45:47.716 align:middle
there hasn't been
a whole lot of talk

45:47.716 --> 45:50.746 align:middle
about central bank digital
currency and wondering

45:50.746 --> 45:53.816 align:middle
if you could give us an update
on where things are with that.

45:53.816 --> 45:55.576 align:middle
Is that considered
a dead issue now?

45:55.576 --> 45:57.856 align:middle
Is it still something
that's being discussed

45:57.856 --> 46:00.506 align:middle
within the Committee, and what,
what's happening with that?

46:00.876 --> 46:01.486 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:01.546 --> 46:05.316 align:middle
It's not something that comes
up at all with the-in the FOMC.

46:05.986 --> 46:09.536 align:middle
So, more broadly, digital
finance is an area that's

46:09.536 --> 46:12.486 align:middle
having-that has really
significant implications

46:12.486 --> 46:15.366 align:middle
for payments generally,
instant payments.

46:15.366 --> 46:16.576 align:middle
And, you know, it's
something that's going

46:16.576 --> 46:18.696 align:middle
to really change
the way-it's going

46:18.696 --> 46:20.166 align:middle
to make more efficient
and, hopefully,

46:20.166 --> 46:23.256 align:middle
safer and all those
things-the way payments are made

46:23.256 --> 46:23.836 align:middle
around the world.

46:23.976 --> 46:26.566 align:middle
And so we have people who are
researching that and trying

46:26.566 --> 46:28.896 align:middle
to keep up to speed, because
we play an important role

46:28.896 --> 46:33.386 align:middle
in the payments sector,
both as a convener

46:33.386 --> 46:34.586 align:middle
and as an operator, too.

46:34.586 --> 46:38.616 align:middle
In terms of a CBDC, there's
really nothing new going on.

46:38.616 --> 46:39.986 align:middle
There's not much
going on at all.

46:40.436 --> 46:42.496 align:middle
We're not-we don't
have the authority

46:42.556 --> 46:47.976 align:middle
to issue a CB-a retail CBDC
that's available to the public.

46:47.976 --> 46:49.316 align:middle
We're not seeking
that authority.

46:49.886 --> 46:53.676 align:middle
So what we're doing is
keeping up with-keeping

46:53.676 --> 46:54.816 align:middle
up with developments there.

46:55.356 --> 46:57.786 align:middle
Pretty much every major
central bank in the world is

46:58.186 --> 46:59.886 align:middle
at least doing, doing that.

46:59.886 --> 47:01.366 align:middle
Some of them are
actually seriously looking

47:01.366 --> 47:02.176 align:middle
at implementing a CBDC.

47:02.176 --> 47:03.526 align:middle
We're really not.

47:04.126 --> 47:07.606 align:middle
We're really just
evaluating, you know, the story

47:07.606 --> 47:09.426 align:middle
and what's happening out there.

47:09.776 --> 47:13.476 align:middle
So I think it's work
that we need to be doing,

47:13.476 --> 47:15.386 align:middle
which could be very
beneficial down the road,

47:15.886 --> 47:19.986 align:middle
but we don't have-on a CBDC,
we don't have any plan to.

47:19.986 --> 47:22.476 align:middle
We would need to go to Congress,
and we have no plan to do that.

47:22.476 --> 47:24.036 align:middle
We're not-no one
here has decided

47:24.036 --> 47:25.516 align:middle
that we think it's
a good idea yet.

47:27.096 --> 47:27.476 align:middle
Thank you.

47:27.646 --> 47:37.446 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

47:42.146 --> 47:43.976 align:middle
Thank you.

